e like Queen c7 or move like 'if I've  breaking in the center taking advantage  of these dark squares so the white  players at the time  well why would I allow all that I can  simply play Bishop f4 stop all of these  moves and force black to do something  else of course the downside to this is  that you give black the g4 square so you  see kind of the give-and-take already in  the game  gingy ice really chose to play Bishop f4  so Karpov developed his Bishop the queen  is attacked so it can comes out to be  three hitting a b7 pawn simply queen c8  defends the threat this Knight comes out  to d2 we now see II 6 Knight gf3 Bishop  e7 castles and castles both sides simply  getting developed and now Rick a e1 was  White's choice this Bishop actually  comes back to h5 and this move might  might look a little bit strange at first  but the ideas is very very simple the  idea is that this bishop in structures  like this even with a pawn on e3  sometimes the LightSquared Bishop is  almost always White's most powerful  minor piece so you'll see black quite  often do anything you can to trade this  Bishop off the board and that's exactly  what's going on here with Bishop h5  Karpov is aiming to play Bishop g6 to  contest this Bishop and force it to  trade itself off white tries to prevent  this by playing Knight e5 keeping an eye  on the g6 square but Karpov is not so  easily swayed he trades this knight and  continues with Bishop g6 anyways white  takes this opportunity not to try to  save the bishop because if the bishop  can't be on d3 it's really not quite as  powerful as White would like it to be so  instead we see the trade doubling  Black's pawns and now this Queen  actually retreats all the way back to d1  and now we kind of fully understand that  the character of the position both sides  that have made some trades made some  decisions already and it's very very  clear what both sides want to do of  course we have a Karlsbad like structure  on the Queen side here meaning white has  these four pawns all lined up seemingly  very very solid and in the meanwhile  black has a deep pond and  and a beep on but no seep on so what  this means is it's the prime opportunity  for black to launch what's called a  minority attack minority attack of  course is when you attack upon majority  with a pawn minority so black is gonna  use these two pawns to try and create  some weaknesses on the queenside  you can expect to move like b4 aiming  for B takes c3 and if this be pawn will  recapture the C pawn is now very very  weak on a half-open file so what does  this leave for white well you can simply  look at the placement of his pieces and  the the idea becomes pretty obvious this  Queen just retreated back to d1 aiming  at the Kings side this rook ends at the  center and more specifically the King  site side of the center this rook  already placed on the F file aims at the  center this Bishop aims at the King this  Knight ready to come in to attack the  King White's gonna play on the Kings  side and so black wastes no time  launches his minority attack with b5  white starts with the move rook III  black continues with a 5 and now white  plays h4 and so both sides are fully  committed to to these plans as they  should be black continues with b4 what  continues with g4 and now it's a very  interesting decision that that Karpov  has to make here there's a couple of  different ways you can treat this  tension on the Queen side one way you  can handle it is to take on c3 and this  would be a perfectly valid way to play  rook takes c3 would likely be the  response and now rather than a weakness  on c3 white has a weakness on b2 to  contend with to imagine move like Queen  a6 and something like King g2 and this  is going to be the the weak point in the  white camp  that that black is going to aim for  however if you notice though this Bishop  is placed on a very nice diagonal which  for the moment prevents any black rooks  from coming straight to b8 so perhaps  this is one reason why our pub chose  another way to deal with this tension  and that is playing a for Karpov wants  to attack the pawn chain at its  wants to play a three get rid of this  annoying b2 pond and then break through  on the C file in the a file which I like  this idea quite a bit it does give a  wipe the opportunity to capture on b4  also gaining this pun structure but it  would activate the black bishop  ever-so-slightly  instead we see h5 white just continues  his attack on the King side and now  Karpov continues with a3 just simply  going right for the throat attacking the  space of the pawn chain as soon as  possible and this is a very interesting  dynamic that you you see when both sides  have plans on opposite sides of the  board that they kind of ignore each  other the reason for that is sometimes  when you touch your pawns or touch your  pieces on the side of the board where  your opponent's attacking you it only  serves to make more weaknesses or to  quicken their play so both sides are  ignoring each other trying to get their  plan to break through first and here we  see white actually go for C takes b4  when in fact a move like H takes g6  might have also been another way to play  just continuing to ignore nor the  threats but we see a takes b4 here  Karpov actually makes a pretty  significant mistake the best move by far  is to take on b2 opening up this a file  and allowing the black pieces to really  be active perhaps he was worried about a  move like a3 defending this b4 upon when  it's a little bit unclear if black is  gonna be in time to hold on to this pawn  and break through on the Queen's side if  they simply move like Knight d7 you're  gonna want to move this Bishop out of  the way and after we we clear up some  tension over here and Bishop f6 it's a  little unclear how white is going to  pick up this pawn when our bishops very  very quickly coming into the game of  course there are other ways to play as  well for example i