en before  being played King g2 trying to use this  h-file rook AC 8 Merc a7 Queen D for  this very very nice central square for  the Queen is available due to the  absence of this dark square Bishop rook  a4 and now the pawns start falling off  the board  or white we see a few more moves g5 this  Queen comes up to be too and really this  this queen is just collecting all of the  pawns and this King comes back to h1 we  see King g7 using the H file now two  blacks advantage Merck a7 Bishop c5 rook  takes f7 was wisp White's choice and now  white simply resigns the game of course  if if white had just moved the Rick out  of the way  Bishop takes f2 would be devastating  with Rick h8 quickly to follow and I  felt like this was a very very  instructive game for for Karpov here you  see both players ignoring each other  playing on the side of the board but on  opposite sides of the board but when it  really counts white missed his  opportunity to lock things down on the  Queen's side so always be on the lookout  in your games for ways to really slow  down your opponent's plan at the right  moment  that being said Karpov broke through won  the game and kudos to him for being the  first maybe unofficial world rapid  champion all the way back in 1988 with  that being said this was game review  thank you very much for joining me my  name is Caleb Denby and I will see you  in the next episode

